WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.742 --> 00:00:03.450
MAYA KACZOROWSKI:
Welcome to Cloud OnAir,

00:00:03.450 --> 00:00:05.330
live webinars from Google Cloud.

00:00:05.330 --> 00:00:07.170
We're hosting webinars
every Tuesday.

00:00:07.170 --> 00:00:08.424
My name is Maya Kaczorowski.

00:00:08.424 --> 00:00:09.840
I'm a product
manager in security.

00:00:09.840 --> 00:00:12.144
And today we'll be talking
about container security.

00:00:12.144 --> 00:00:14.060
You can ask questions
anytime on the platform.

00:00:14.060 --> 00:00:16.630
And we have Googlers on
standby to answer them.

00:00:16.630 --> 00:00:19.122
So let's get started.

00:00:19.122 --> 00:00:21.080
So we'll cover a couple
different topics today.

00:00:21.080 --> 00:00:23.780
First we'll start with, kind
of, container security 101.

00:00:23.780 --> 00:00:26.160
Why are containers different
to secure than VMs are?

00:00:26.160 --> 00:00:28.160
Then we'll talk about the
secure software supply

00:00:28.160 --> 00:00:30.980
chain and deep dive into two
of our newest products, Google

00:00:30.980 --> 00:00:32.960
Container Registry
Vulnerability Scanning

00:00:32.960 --> 00:00:34.700
and binary authorization.

00:00:34.700 --> 00:00:36.470
Lastly, we'll see what else you
should be doing in this space,

00:00:36.470 --> 00:00:37.790
beyond software supply chain.

00:00:37.790 --> 00:00:39.456
How else can you
protect your containers

00:00:39.456 --> 00:00:41.460
running in production?

00:00:41.460 --> 00:00:44.007
So starting off with
container security 101.

00:00:44.007 --> 00:00:45.840
A lot of what's needed
to protect containers

00:00:45.840 --> 00:00:48.510
are constructs that we're
already familiar with.

00:00:48.510 --> 00:00:50.640
They're similar to
things we have VMs.

00:00:50.640 --> 00:00:54.500
We still want firewalls, access
management, audit logging, IDS,

00:00:54.500 --> 00:00:55.429
IPS, and more.

00:00:55.429 --> 00:00:57.720
The concepts that you need
for securing your containers

00:00:57.720 --> 00:00:59.200
aren't dramatically different.

00:00:59.200 --> 00:01:01.533
But the mindset of constantly
rebuilding and redeploying

00:01:01.533 --> 00:01:03.174
containers is what's changed.

00:01:03.174 --> 00:01:04.590
So what specifically
is different?

00:01:04.590 --> 00:01:07.620
Let's get that out of the way
first in terms of how security

00:01:07.620 --> 00:01:09.670
is implemented for containers.

00:01:09.670 --> 00:01:10.970
First is the surface of attack.

00:01:10.970 --> 00:01:12.720
A container bundles
binaries and libraries

00:01:12.720 --> 00:01:14.187
as part of the container image.

00:01:14.187 --> 00:01:16.020
So you don't need as
much in the host image.

00:01:16.020 --> 00:01:17.674
If you're using
containers as intended,

00:01:17.674 --> 00:01:19.590
that host OS doesn't
have as much bloat, which

00:01:19.590 --> 00:01:22.200
reduces the surface of attack.

00:01:22.200 --> 00:01:24.930
Next, resource isolation--
this is built into containers,

00:01:24.930 --> 00:01:26.850
allowing you to easily
allocate resources,

00:01:26.850 --> 00:01:30.590
like storage volumes, to certain
processes using namespaces.

00:01:30.590 --> 00:01:32.090
In terms of
provisioning, containers

00:01:32.090 --> 00:01:33.673
have normal access
controls like you'd

00:01:33.673 --> 00:01:35.990
expect for controlling
access to shared resources

00:01:35.990 --> 00:01:37.790
and restricting
apps' privileges.

00:01:37.790 --> 00:01:40.430
That being said, containers by
construction are more powerful

00:01:40.430 --> 00:01:41.888
and have a wider
range of sys calls

00:01:41.888 --> 00:01:43.346
that they can form the kernel.

00:01:43.346 --> 00:01:44.720
And lastly,
lifetime-- containers

00:01:44.720 --> 00:01:47.053
are meant to be immutable and
are frequently redeployed.

00:01:47.053 --> 00:01:48.750
So they're fairly short lived.

00:01:48.750 --> 00:01:51.100
In fact, 95% of containers
live less than a week.

00:01:51.100 --> 00:01:52.850
This should make it
harder for an attacker

00:01:52.850 --> 00:01:54.260
to persist in your environment.

00:01:54.260 --> 00:01:56.307
But it also means that
if something does go bad,

00:01:56.307 --> 00:01:58.640
by the time that you realize
it, the effective container

00:01:58.640 --> 00:02:00.098
might not be there
anymore, and you

00:02:00.098 --> 00:02:02.659
won't be able to do forensics.

00:02:02.659 --> 00:02:04.700
So what kinds of threats
are there to containers?

00:02:04.700 --> 00:02:06.600
What are we seeing in the wild?

00:02:06.600 --> 00:02:08.419
How do we categorize
these threats?

00:02:08.419 --> 00:02:10.850
First is the set of threats
to container peripherals,

00:02:10.850 --> 00:02:13.160
like credential compromise
and privilege escalation.

00:02:13.160 --> 00:02:15.618
This is about how to get the
Kubernetes API to do something

00:02:15.618 --> 00:02:17.254
that you might
not want it to do.

00:02:17.254 --> 00:02:19.420
Next is what ends up running
in your infrastructure.

00:02:19.420 --> 00:02:21.586
You might be pulling images
from public repositories

00:02:21.586 --> 00:02:23.887
or have dependencies on
unsupported open source code.

00:02:23.887 --> 00:02:25.970
If there's a new zero day
vulnerability discovered

00:02:25.970 --> 00:02:28.190
tomorrow, how do you figure
out if you're affected,

00:02:28.190 --> 00:02:30.150
and how do you quickly
patch yourself?

00:02:30.150 --> 00:02:31.700
And there are things that we
hear people get really scared

00:02:31.700 --> 00:02:33.980
about, things like DOS attacks
and flooding your event

00:02:33.980 --> 00:02:36.929
pipeline, or even
container escapes.

00:02:36.929 --> 00:02:39.220
And this is how we're thinking
about container security

00:02:39.220 --> 00:02:39.792
at Google.

00:02:39.792 --> 00:02:41.500
If you think about
what kinds of controls

00:02:41.500 --> 00:02:43.840
we can put in place for each
of these types of threats,

00:02:43.840 --> 00:02:45.100
we end up with
three main pillars--

00:02:45.100 --> 00:02:46.660
how you develop your
applications, how you make sure

00:02:46.660 --> 00:02:48.670
your images are good
to build and deploy,

00:02:48.670 --> 00:02:50.128
and how you protect
your containers

00:02:50.128 --> 00:02:51.070
while they're running.

00:02:51.070 --> 00:02:52.780
So the first one there,
infrastructure security,

00:02:52.780 --> 00:02:54.130
is about ensuring that
your developers have

00:02:54.130 --> 00:02:56.170
the tools that they
need to securely build

00:02:56.170 --> 00:02:57.670
containerized services.

00:02:57.670 --> 00:03:00.220
This includes identity and
access management, logging,

00:03:00.220 --> 00:03:03.589
secret management,
networking, other such tools.

00:03:03.589 --> 00:03:05.380
The software supply
chain is about ensuring

00:03:05.380 --> 00:03:06.796
that you know
exactly what's being

00:03:06.796 --> 00:03:09.364
deployed in your environment
and that it belongs there.

00:03:09.364 --> 00:03:11.530
This is about securing the
host image, the container

00:03:11.530 --> 00:03:14.330
image, and the
application code itself.

00:03:14.330 --> 00:03:16.330
Lastly, runtime security
is about ensuring

00:03:16.330 --> 00:03:18.880
that your security response
team can detect and respond

00:03:18.880 --> 00:03:20.590
to security threats
to containers

00:03:20.590 --> 00:03:21.907
running in your environment.

00:03:21.907 --> 00:03:24.240
So today we're going to focus
on that middle piece here,

00:03:24.240 --> 00:03:25.820
the secured software
supply chain.

00:03:28.550 --> 00:03:30.830
So even if how you secure
containers is actually

00:03:30.830 --> 00:03:33.121
very similar to VMs, except
for some of the differences

00:03:33.121 --> 00:03:35.570
that we pointed out, running
containerized microservices

00:03:35.570 --> 00:03:37.094
leads to a different
security model.

00:03:37.094 --> 00:03:39.260
You have a fundamentally
different mindset to adopt,

00:03:39.260 --> 00:03:42.290
which arguably actually leads
to better security longer term.

00:03:42.290 --> 00:03:44.390
First off, containers are
meant to be short lived

00:03:44.390 --> 00:03:45.890
and frequently redeployed.

00:03:45.890 --> 00:03:48.140
Rather than pushing a
single line of code change,

00:03:48.140 --> 00:03:49.834
rebuild and redeploy
the whole image.

00:03:49.834 --> 00:03:52.250
If you have other processes
that can take place passively,

00:03:52.250 --> 00:03:53.940
like patching your
OS for example,

00:03:53.940 --> 00:03:55.070
you can be constantly
doing these.

00:03:55.070 --> 00:03:57.278
You don't have to have any
Sunday 2:00 AM maintenance

00:03:57.278 --> 00:03:58.280
windows.

00:03:58.280 --> 00:04:00.207
Containers are also
meant to be immutable.

00:04:00.207 --> 00:04:01.790
This is related to
the previous point.

00:04:01.790 --> 00:04:03.623
So you're not changing
single lines of code.

00:04:03.623 --> 00:04:05.060
You're changing
whole containers.

00:04:05.060 --> 00:04:06.740
What that means is that you
have a single chokepoint

00:04:06.740 --> 00:04:07.760
for everything
that gets deployed

00:04:07.760 --> 00:04:08.970
into your infrastructure.

00:04:08.970 --> 00:04:10.760
If you're deploying frequently,
you can actually really

00:04:10.760 --> 00:04:12.260
quickly see and
control what's being

00:04:12.260 --> 00:04:14.050
run in your infrastructure.

00:04:14.050 --> 00:04:15.425
A common concern
about containers

00:04:15.425 --> 00:04:18.230
is they don't provide a strong
way to contain workloads,

00:04:18.230 --> 00:04:20.120
or rather, VMs have
a strong boundary,

00:04:20.120 --> 00:04:22.056
like a hypervisor,
that's well understood.

00:04:22.056 --> 00:04:23.930
As a result, users are
hesitant about running

00:04:23.930 --> 00:04:25.550
multi-tenant environments,
that is deploying

00:04:25.550 --> 00:04:27.950
two containers for different
workloads on the same VM

00:04:27.950 --> 00:04:30.980
because they're worried about
one workload affecting another.

00:04:30.980 --> 00:04:33.254
This is a fair concern
but no longer really true

00:04:33.254 --> 00:04:33.920
with containers.

00:04:33.920 --> 00:04:36.336
We now have technologies like
gVisor that are specifically

00:04:36.336 --> 00:04:37.760
built to remediate this risk.

00:04:37.760 --> 00:04:39.676
So the primary counter
argument for containers

00:04:39.676 --> 00:04:41.620
is actually now one of
its greatest strengths.

00:04:41.620 --> 00:04:43.703
And lastly, containers and
orchestration platforms

00:04:43.703 --> 00:04:45.890
like Kubernetes have
security features built in.

00:04:45.890 --> 00:04:48.389
A lot of security changes, when
they're not already enabled,

00:04:48.389 --> 00:04:49.680
are simple config changes.

00:04:49.680 --> 00:04:52.020
You don't need to go
build a new tool yourself

00:04:52.020 --> 00:04:53.545
in order to
[INAUDIBLE] something.

00:04:53.545 --> 00:04:54.920
So altogether it
means that, yes,

00:04:54.920 --> 00:04:56.419
that your mental
model is different.

00:04:56.419 --> 00:04:59.250
But if you actually have much
stronger security properties.

00:04:59.250 --> 00:05:02.060
So as we talk about the secure
software supply chain today,

00:05:02.060 --> 00:05:04.437
we're really focusing
on the first two pieces.

00:05:04.437 --> 00:05:06.020
Let's look at those
a bit more deeply.

00:05:06.020 --> 00:05:07.700
The fact that we have
short-lived, frequently

00:05:07.700 --> 00:05:10.460
redeployed containers, and that
they're meant to be immutable,

00:05:10.460 --> 00:05:12.350
together this dictates
a lot of what goes on

00:05:12.350 --> 00:05:15.029
in your software supply chain.

00:05:15.029 --> 00:05:16.320
How is this actually different?

00:05:16.320 --> 00:05:18.875
Well, historically, a monolithic
application running on a VM,

00:05:18.875 --> 00:05:20.250
to make a change,
your developers

00:05:20.250 --> 00:05:21.750
would need to SSH
into that machine

00:05:21.750 --> 00:05:23.910
or push code changes without
necessarily predicting

00:05:23.910 --> 00:05:25.720
the impact that would have on
the rest of your environment,

00:05:25.720 --> 00:05:27.390
what you see on the left there.

00:05:27.390 --> 00:05:29.681
There are manual adjustments,
including for maintenance

00:05:29.681 --> 00:05:30.962
operations, like restarts.

00:05:30.962 --> 00:05:32.670
With containers, you
can have a pipeline.

00:05:32.670 --> 00:05:34.860
You can push code to images,
which then go through

00:05:34.860 --> 00:05:37.260
your design requirements
for build, test, scan,

00:05:37.260 --> 00:05:39.370
et cetera, until deployment.

00:05:39.370 --> 00:05:42.270
Micro services are redeployed
as individual container images

00:05:42.270 --> 00:05:44.460
rather than changing
specific lines of code.

00:05:44.460 --> 00:05:48.556
It's easier to debug, and
it's easier to change.

00:05:48.556 --> 00:05:50.930
And so from your code, you
might create a base image that

00:05:50.930 --> 00:05:52.030
goes through your pipeline.

00:05:52.030 --> 00:05:53.330
What we're going
to focus on today

00:05:53.330 --> 00:05:54.830
are two items that
you can integrate

00:05:54.830 --> 00:05:56.900
into your pipeline between
developing your code

00:05:56.900 --> 00:05:58.460
and pushing into production.

00:05:58.460 --> 00:06:00.752
The first is Container Registry
Vulnerability Scanning.

00:06:00.752 --> 00:06:03.084
You might want to store a
certain image metadata as part

00:06:03.084 --> 00:06:05.210
of your image repository,
including, notably,

00:06:05.210 --> 00:06:07.550
whether your images have
known vulnerabilities.

00:06:07.550 --> 00:06:09.470
And the second one is
binary authorization.

00:06:09.470 --> 00:06:10.880
You might want to enforce
that your images meet

00:06:10.880 --> 00:06:13.463
certain requirements before you
deploy them to your production

00:06:13.463 --> 00:06:14.400
environment.

00:06:14.400 --> 00:06:15.590
So let's dig into this.

00:06:15.590 --> 00:06:17.870
First up, Container Registry
Vulnerability Scanning.

00:06:22.575 --> 00:06:24.200
JUAN SEBASTIAN OVIEDO:
Thank you, Maya.

00:06:24.200 --> 00:06:24.960
Hello, everyone.

00:06:24.960 --> 00:06:26.959
I'm going to talk [INAUDIBLE]
Container Registry

00:06:26.959 --> 00:06:29.744
Vulnerability Scanning.

00:06:29.744 --> 00:06:32.160
Essentially what Container
Registry Vulnerability Scanning

00:06:32.160 --> 00:06:36.510
does is it scans your images
in Google Container Registry

00:06:36.510 --> 00:06:39.210
for known vulnerabilities
by checking

00:06:39.210 --> 00:06:41.380
the corresponding
security databases.

00:06:41.380 --> 00:06:43.500
The value in this
is that enables

00:06:43.500 --> 00:06:46.620
you to identify vulnerabilities
before deployment.

00:06:46.620 --> 00:06:48.455
So you can address
them and in that way

00:06:48.455 --> 00:06:50.580
protect your application
and protect your customers

00:06:50.580 --> 00:06:52.230
and your infrastructure.

00:06:52.230 --> 00:06:56.820
Currently, the product
supports Debian, Ubuntu,

00:06:56.820 --> 00:06:59.460
and Alpine-based images
by reactively working

00:06:59.460 --> 00:07:01.740
on any support
[INAUDIBLE] distributions,

00:07:01.740 --> 00:07:05.040
like CentOS and Red Hat.

00:07:05.040 --> 00:07:09.060
What it does is once
vulnerabilities are found,

00:07:09.060 --> 00:07:12.000
alerts are generated using
our Pub/Sub integration.

00:07:12.000 --> 00:07:17.370
And they also surface in our
UI, our CLI, and also VR APIs.

00:07:17.370 --> 00:07:20.940
Images are scanned as soon
as you push them into GCR.

00:07:20.940 --> 00:07:23.790
And also, after
that initial scan,

00:07:23.790 --> 00:07:25.410
we continuously analyze them.

00:07:25.410 --> 00:07:27.360
And as new vulnerabilities
are discovered,

00:07:27.360 --> 00:07:31.160
we surface that
information for you.

00:07:31.160 --> 00:07:36.440
So once you find a vulnerability
using Vulnerability Scanner,

00:07:36.440 --> 00:07:37.710
how should you react?

00:07:37.710 --> 00:07:38.750
What should you do?

00:07:38.750 --> 00:07:40.850
Well, as I mentioned,
the information

00:07:40.850 --> 00:07:44.015
is made easily available
for you via the UI, the CLI,

00:07:44.015 --> 00:07:45.710
and the API.

00:07:45.710 --> 00:07:48.830
You can also use something
like the GCP mobile application

00:07:48.830 --> 00:07:51.290
to set up push notifications
so you're alerted when

00:07:51.290 --> 00:07:54.290
your vulnerabilities are found.

00:07:54.290 --> 00:07:56.420
Also, with Pub/Sub,
this gives you

00:07:56.420 --> 00:07:58.910
a lot of power in terms
of plug in the findings

00:07:58.910 --> 00:08:00.630
and plug in the
vulnerability information

00:08:00.630 --> 00:08:02.450
into other parts
of your workflow.

00:08:02.450 --> 00:08:06.570
And you can use some Cloud
Functions to essentially enable

00:08:06.570 --> 00:08:09.390
a communication between
these different systems.

00:08:09.390 --> 00:08:12.320
An example could be using
the Pub/Sub notification

00:08:12.320 --> 00:08:15.190
information via Cloud
Functions to then generate

00:08:15.190 --> 00:08:18.967
an issue or an item
to track in your repo

00:08:18.967 --> 00:08:21.050
so that someone is assigned
to that vulnerability,

00:08:21.050 --> 00:08:25.480
and it can be further
analyzed and addressed.

00:08:25.480 --> 00:08:28.255
In terms of how to
mitigate vulnerabilities

00:08:28.255 --> 00:08:30.500
and how to prevent them
in the first place,

00:08:30.500 --> 00:08:32.750
there are three
general categories.

00:08:32.750 --> 00:08:35.950
The first category is basically
updating your packages.

00:08:35.950 --> 00:08:38.419
So this is easier
said than done.

00:08:38.419 --> 00:08:41.049
But it's very important
for you to regularly update

00:08:41.049 --> 00:08:44.290
the components that you use
to build your images as part

00:08:44.290 --> 00:08:47.650
of your Docker build images
and your general build process,

00:08:47.650 --> 00:08:51.490
something as simple as getUpdate
and getUpgrade goes a long way

00:08:51.490 --> 00:08:52.390
during that.

00:08:52.390 --> 00:08:54.700
All components are updated,
and you're not exposed

00:08:54.700 --> 00:08:57.220
to previous vulnerabilities.

00:08:57.220 --> 00:08:59.830
Category number two
is removing packages.

00:08:59.830 --> 00:09:03.520
Essentially, if you
use a stock base image

00:09:03.520 --> 00:09:07.180
from Ubuntu or Debian, those
come in with a large set

00:09:07.180 --> 00:09:08.020
of packages.

00:09:08.020 --> 00:09:10.750
And many times your application
wouldn't need all of those

00:09:10.750 --> 00:09:11.355
to run.

00:09:11.355 --> 00:09:13.480
So it's important for you
to analyze and understand

00:09:13.480 --> 00:09:15.265
what packages do I
really need and then

00:09:15.265 --> 00:09:17.780
to remove the ones
that are not necessary.

00:09:17.780 --> 00:09:21.290
And then the third category
is using a new distribution.

00:09:21.290 --> 00:09:22.830
For example, Alpine
and Debian Slim

00:09:22.830 --> 00:09:25.100
are much lighter
and much smaller.

00:09:25.100 --> 00:09:27.640
And by using one
of those, if you're

00:09:27.640 --> 00:09:29.440
able to run your
application on those,

00:09:29.440 --> 00:09:32.260
the attack surface is
significantly reduced,

00:09:32.260 --> 00:09:34.120
and your software
supply chain can

00:09:34.120 --> 00:09:38.960
be secure much more effectively
by using one of those.

00:09:38.960 --> 00:09:40.960
So in terms of Container
Registry Vulnerability

00:09:40.960 --> 00:09:44.950
Scanning, to use it, we're
currently in our first unit

00:09:44.950 --> 00:09:49.360
to join the Container
Analysis Google Group.

00:09:49.360 --> 00:09:51.940
This is going to change
soon, once we go to beta.

00:09:51.940 --> 00:09:56.110
But basically what you'll do
is you need to enable the API.

00:09:56.110 --> 00:09:59.320
And after you do that, you'll
start to see information,

00:09:59.320 --> 00:10:02.140
like basically your
images will be scanned.

00:10:02.140 --> 00:10:04.210
You'll see a status
change to scanning.

00:10:04.210 --> 00:10:05.630
And once the process
is completed,

00:10:05.630 --> 00:10:07.540
you'll be able to
look over the results

00:10:07.540 --> 00:10:09.850
and find the number of
vulnerabilities in a given

00:10:09.850 --> 00:10:14.480
image, like in the
image in the slide.

00:10:14.480 --> 00:10:18.410
In terms of some basically key
definitions for vulnerability

00:10:18.410 --> 00:10:22.380
scanning, a note is a detailed
description of a vulnerability.

00:10:22.380 --> 00:10:27.040
So for example, the severity
of it, the severity score,

00:10:27.040 --> 00:10:29.480
and occurrence is
information as to where

00:10:29.480 --> 00:10:31.000
it happened, where
it was found, so

00:10:31.000 --> 00:10:32.930
for example, what
package it applies to.

00:10:32.930 --> 00:10:35.529
And a vulnerability
result is the summary

00:10:35.529 --> 00:10:37.070
of the different
vulnerabilities that

00:10:37.070 --> 00:10:40.392
were found in a corresponding
image, basically the summary

00:10:40.392 --> 00:10:42.350
result that you see in
the middle of the screen

00:10:42.350 --> 00:10:44.700
there in the slide.

00:10:44.700 --> 00:10:46.520
So I'm going to give
you a quick demo

00:10:46.520 --> 00:10:49.440
of the vulnerability scanning
UI and walk you through it.

00:10:55.770 --> 00:10:59.630
So here we're starting
from the console.

00:10:59.630 --> 00:11:01.310
And then what I'll
do here is I'll

00:11:01.310 --> 00:11:04.030
search for Container Registry.

00:11:04.030 --> 00:11:07.270
Once I go there, I'll be able to
see all of the different images

00:11:07.270 --> 00:11:08.890
that I've pushed.

00:11:08.890 --> 00:11:11.810
So as you can see here, I have
different categories of images.

00:11:11.810 --> 00:11:14.530
I'm going through the
Python-based images.

00:11:14.530 --> 00:11:17.950
And in there you'll see that
I've pushed three images.

00:11:17.950 --> 00:11:20.884
The bottom one, you
start Debian as the base.

00:11:20.884 --> 00:11:22.300
And as you can see
in the summary,

00:11:22.300 --> 00:11:25.000
there are five vulnerabilities
where fixes were found,

00:11:25.000 --> 00:11:28.270
and a total of 509
vulnerabilities were found.

00:11:28.270 --> 00:11:30.940
In the Summary page, you
can see this scan results

00:11:30.940 --> 00:11:34.450
at the top, which give you the
counts for the total, the ones

00:11:34.450 --> 00:11:36.090
that have fixes available.

00:11:36.090 --> 00:11:38.170
That accounts for
different severities.

00:11:38.170 --> 00:11:40.030
And then you can
scroll down the list.

00:11:40.030 --> 00:11:44.270
And in each row you can find
the severity, the score,

00:11:44.270 --> 00:11:45.820
whether a fix is
available or not,

00:11:45.820 --> 00:11:47.624
the package that it
applies to, and also

00:11:47.624 --> 00:11:49.790
a link to the documentation
where you can learn more

00:11:49.790 --> 00:11:51.330
about the vulnerability.

00:11:51.330 --> 00:11:52.900
Something also you
can do is you can

00:11:52.900 --> 00:11:54.430
scroll through the
different pages

00:11:54.430 --> 00:11:55.840
to find all of the information.

00:11:55.840 --> 00:11:57.877
And you can also sort by column.

00:11:57.877 --> 00:12:00.460
So for example, in this case, I
sorted for the vulnerabilities

00:12:00.460 --> 00:12:03.230
that have fixes available.

00:12:03.230 --> 00:12:06.370
And then when you click in
a specific vulnerability,

00:12:06.370 --> 00:12:08.344
you'll be able to
see details about it.

00:12:08.344 --> 00:12:09.760
So for example,
you'll see when it

00:12:09.760 --> 00:12:12.910
was created, when it
was updated, details,

00:12:12.910 --> 00:12:14.380
how the occurrence, the note.

00:12:14.380 --> 00:12:16.390
And importantly,
you also see links

00:12:16.390 --> 00:12:19.285
to documentation for the
specific vulnerabilities.

00:12:19.285 --> 00:12:22.750
So you learn what remediation
steps you can take.

00:12:22.750 --> 00:12:25.030
Now, going back, the
second image that I pushed,

00:12:25.030 --> 00:12:26.951
you start Debian Slim base.

00:12:26.951 --> 00:12:29.200
So as you can see, this one
has fewer vulnerabilities,

00:12:29.200 --> 00:12:32.470
51 in total, and none
of them with fixes.

00:12:32.470 --> 00:12:35.710
And as I showed you before, when
I go through the list of items,

00:12:35.710 --> 00:12:39.550
I can see the overall counts,
rows for each vulnerability.

00:12:39.550 --> 00:12:41.140
And clicking on each
of them gives me

00:12:41.140 --> 00:12:44.500
more details in terms
of what I should

00:12:44.500 --> 00:12:47.720
do by going to the vulnerability
documentation page.

00:12:47.720 --> 00:12:52.700
And then the last image that
I pushed was based on Alpine.

00:12:52.700 --> 00:12:55.150
And as I mentioned, Alpine is
a much smaller distribution,

00:12:55.150 --> 00:12:56.080
much lighter.

00:12:56.080 --> 00:13:00.290
So in this case, no known
vulnerabilities were found.

00:13:00.290 --> 00:13:03.520
So what you can see here
is, as you go through it,

00:13:03.520 --> 00:13:06.400
basically when you push
images, they are analyzed.

00:13:06.400 --> 00:13:08.680
The information is
made available for you.

00:13:08.680 --> 00:13:13.480
And very importantly, you are
given information on actions

00:13:13.480 --> 00:13:16.450
that you could take by
going into that Details page

00:13:16.450 --> 00:13:19.600
on potentially how you can
address a vulnerability.

00:13:19.600 --> 00:13:22.370
Something else that I
want to cover is Grafeas.

00:13:22.370 --> 00:13:27.100
Grafeas is an open
artifact metadata API

00:13:27.100 --> 00:13:29.020
that we've worked on
with the community.

00:13:29.020 --> 00:13:31.690
What it does is it enables
you to have a consistent way

00:13:31.690 --> 00:13:34.540
to store information
about the artifacts

00:13:34.540 --> 00:13:36.820
and the general items
in your build process

00:13:36.820 --> 00:13:38.470
so that you can reason about it.

00:13:38.470 --> 00:13:40.554
Essentially, you can know
what artifacts you have,

00:13:40.554 --> 00:13:42.261
whether or not they
have vulnerabilities.

00:13:42.261 --> 00:13:43.750
You can sort that information.

00:13:43.750 --> 00:13:45.509
And then you can
also know what's

00:13:45.509 --> 00:13:47.800
running in production, what's
in different environments

00:13:47.800 --> 00:13:50.008
and based on that information
make informed decisions

00:13:50.008 --> 00:13:52.590
to protect your supply chain.

00:13:52.590 --> 00:13:56.140
As I mentioned, this is
something that is open.

00:13:56.140 --> 00:13:59.230
So you can add new metadata
types and providers

00:13:59.230 --> 00:14:02.080
as your supply chain
evolves and grows.

00:14:02.080 --> 00:14:05.140
It's an open standard that we've
worked on with contributors,

00:14:05.140 --> 00:14:10.000
like JFrog, Red Hat, IBM, Black
Duck, Twistlock, Aqua Security,

00:14:10.000 --> 00:14:11.770
CoreOS, among others.

00:14:11.770 --> 00:14:14.225
It gives you full visibility
into the artifacts

00:14:14.225 --> 00:14:16.760
that are going through your
supply chain and their state

00:14:16.760 --> 00:14:18.400
and different metadata.

00:14:18.400 --> 00:14:21.100
And you can set up an
instance to run on premises.

00:14:21.100 --> 00:14:24.310
But then also, our
Container Analysis API

00:14:24.310 --> 00:14:27.280
for Vulnerability Scanner
is an implementation

00:14:27.280 --> 00:14:30.890
of the Grafeas open API.

00:14:30.890 --> 00:14:34.000
And you can learn more about it
by going to Kelsey Hightower's

00:14:34.000 --> 00:14:35.420
tutorial on GitHub.

00:14:35.420 --> 00:14:39.120
You can learn how to set up your
own instance and how to run it.

00:14:39.120 --> 00:14:42.869
And now Sandra's going to talk
about binary authorization.

00:14:51.911 --> 00:14:52.660
SANDRA GUO: Hello.

00:14:52.660 --> 00:14:53.620
My name is Sandra.

00:14:53.620 --> 00:14:57.520
I'm here to talk about
binary authorization.

00:14:57.520 --> 00:15:01.210
So as Maya mentioned earlier,
that containers give you

00:15:01.210 --> 00:15:03.760
a way to apply
consistent changes

00:15:03.760 --> 00:15:07.990
and avoid on-demand direct
manipulation of running

00:15:07.990 --> 00:15:09.650
production jobs.

00:15:09.650 --> 00:15:13.270
So in the container model,
developers no longer directly

00:15:13.270 --> 00:15:15.640
patch updates,
restart running jobs.

00:15:15.640 --> 00:15:19.090
Instead, organizations
can have a consistent way

00:15:19.090 --> 00:15:22.700
to apply changes to any
production workload,

00:15:22.700 --> 00:15:26.950
be it modifications
or new deployment.

00:15:26.950 --> 00:15:31.960
A lot of organizations have
implemented streamlined tests

00:15:31.960 --> 00:15:35.920
as part of their CI/CD pipelines
to apply security, as well

00:15:35.920 --> 00:15:41.610
as integrity tests to make
sure that the changes made

00:15:41.610 --> 00:15:45.920
in production meets
their requirement.

00:15:45.920 --> 00:15:50.620
However, this approach still
does not change the fact

00:15:50.620 --> 00:15:55.360
that deployment controls
is identity based, i.e.,

00:15:55.360 --> 00:15:58.330
restricting who can
deploy, not restricting

00:15:58.330 --> 00:16:01.040
what can be deployed.

00:16:01.040 --> 00:16:03.910
So in this setup,
organizations are still

00:16:03.910 --> 00:16:09.090
vulnerable to deployment
of untrusted code.

00:16:09.090 --> 00:16:11.820
A developer could
still potentially build

00:16:11.820 --> 00:16:14.760
a container outside
of the secure

00:16:14.760 --> 00:16:18.120
build environment and deploy
it on production environment.

00:16:18.120 --> 00:16:21.330
As long as the developer
has permission to deploy,

00:16:21.330 --> 00:16:24.570
this untrusted code will
still go through and affect

00:16:24.570 --> 00:16:27.190
running production jobs.

00:16:27.190 --> 00:16:29.240
And that's where binary
authorization comes in.

00:16:29.240 --> 00:16:33.040
Binary authorization is a
deploy time policy enforcement

00:16:33.040 --> 00:16:36.970
that uses signature
verification to allow

00:16:36.970 --> 00:16:39.220
you to define what
can be deployed,

00:16:39.220 --> 00:16:43.450
in addition to restricting
who can deploy code.

00:16:43.450 --> 00:16:50.350
It integrates with the
GKE Deployment API.

00:16:50.350 --> 00:16:53.290
And it supports both signature
verification, as well as

00:16:53.290 --> 00:16:56.960
image whitelisting to
support various use cases,

00:16:56.960 --> 00:16:59.770
such as making sure
that images are

00:16:59.770 --> 00:17:03.275
built by trusted CI/CD pipeline
before getting deployed

00:17:03.275 --> 00:17:06.660
in production environment,
or only deploy images

00:17:06.660 --> 00:17:10.089
that pass certain
security or quality tests,

00:17:10.089 --> 00:17:14.965
or only deploy explicitly
authorized third-party images.

00:17:17.560 --> 00:17:19.420
Let's take a look
at how it works.

00:17:19.420 --> 00:17:23.109
Binary authorization
works by integrating

00:17:23.109 --> 00:17:29.620
with certain CI/CD stages and
record a signature, or what

00:17:29.620 --> 00:17:32.510
we call attestation, as
the image passes through

00:17:32.510 --> 00:17:35.500
with the particular
CI/CD stage successfully.

00:17:35.500 --> 00:17:39.580
So in this example, the builder
would issue an attestation

00:17:39.580 --> 00:17:43.240
signifying that the image
was built successfully

00:17:43.240 --> 00:17:45.010
by the trusted builder.

00:17:45.010 --> 00:17:47.620
Similarity, tests
would sign, or attest

00:17:47.620 --> 00:17:51.940
the image, if the image
passes necessary tests,

00:17:51.940 --> 00:17:53.880
as well as
vulnerability scanning.

00:17:53.880 --> 00:17:58.120
If the image has passed
necessary vulnerability

00:17:58.120 --> 00:18:00.400
scanning checks,
the scanner would

00:18:00.400 --> 00:18:02.950
issue another attestation.

00:18:02.950 --> 00:18:06.520
As the time the image
comes to deployment,

00:18:06.520 --> 00:18:10.630
there are a set of evidences
that's already accumulated

00:18:10.630 --> 00:18:14.050
for the image that customers
can use to define policies

00:18:14.050 --> 00:18:17.710
and codify what requirement
must the image meet

00:18:17.710 --> 00:18:21.755
before it can enter the
production environment.

00:18:21.755 --> 00:18:26.530
In this model, an
authorized developer

00:18:26.530 --> 00:18:29.780
can try to deploy something
that does not have

00:18:29.780 --> 00:18:31.480
the necessary attestations.

00:18:31.480 --> 00:18:34.250
But this would fail,
and an annotation

00:18:34.250 --> 00:18:38.660
would be added in the audit
log for this deployment attempt

00:18:38.660 --> 00:18:44.640
so that security team can
review this incident later on.

00:18:44.640 --> 00:18:47.270
We understand that
organizations don't just

00:18:47.270 --> 00:18:49.080
want in-house built softwares.

00:18:49.080 --> 00:18:51.060
A lot of the existing
vulnerabilities

00:18:51.060 --> 00:18:55.080
are found in
third-party software.

00:18:55.080 --> 00:18:58.190
Binary authorization
supports this

00:18:58.190 --> 00:19:02.420
by giving organization
security teams a tool

00:19:02.420 --> 00:19:06.980
to define white list of what
third-party images are allowed

00:19:06.980 --> 00:19:09.440
in their production environment
so that they can have

00:19:09.440 --> 00:19:12.710
centralized control
over the software,

00:19:12.710 --> 00:19:16.460
as well as different versions
of the software deployed

00:19:16.460 --> 00:19:20.000
on the organization's
infrastructure.

00:19:20.000 --> 00:19:21.760
So in this example,
the security team

00:19:21.760 --> 00:19:24.580
would define white
list, only allowing

00:19:24.580 --> 00:19:28.560
a particular version of Nginx
that they deem as secure.

00:19:28.560 --> 00:19:34.280
So when a developer deploys
this allowed version of Nginx,

00:19:34.280 --> 00:19:36.560
the deployment goes
through because it

00:19:36.560 --> 00:19:38.420
is allowed by the white list.

00:19:38.420 --> 00:19:42.350
But if a developer, either
malicious or accidentally

00:19:42.350 --> 00:19:45.020
deploy something
that is outdated,

00:19:45.020 --> 00:19:48.490
not allowed by the security
team, the deployment will fail.

00:19:48.490 --> 00:19:50.870
Again, an annotation would
be added in the audit

00:19:50.870 --> 00:19:52.320
log for future review.

00:19:55.771 --> 00:20:01.740
Binary authorization is
integrated in Kubernetes Engine

00:20:01.740 --> 00:20:03.110
and is now in beta.

00:20:03.110 --> 00:20:08.250
To turn on, you first enable
the Binary Authorization API.

00:20:08.250 --> 00:20:11.610
And then when you create or
update your Kubernetes Engine

00:20:11.610 --> 00:20:17.190
clusters, you would turn the
Binary Authorization option on.

00:20:17.190 --> 00:20:20.880
Then you will go ahead and
define your deployment rules

00:20:20.880 --> 00:20:24.390
and Binary Authorization
policy either at the project

00:20:24.390 --> 00:20:27.630
level, which would apply to
all clusters in the project,

00:20:27.630 --> 00:20:31.020
or you can also define
granular cluster-level policy

00:20:31.020 --> 00:20:33.570
override that
would define policy

00:20:33.570 --> 00:20:35.060
for a particular cluster.

00:20:37.620 --> 00:20:40.250
We also support
image whitelisting,

00:20:40.250 --> 00:20:47.030
either using a repository,
a particular path,

00:20:47.030 --> 00:20:51.020
a pattern of the
image name, a path,

00:20:51.020 --> 00:20:53.180
or a particular set
of image digests.

00:20:56.130 --> 00:21:01.650
As I mentioned, any violation
of the deployment policy

00:21:01.650 --> 00:21:05.880
failed attempt would be
recorded in the audit log

00:21:05.880 --> 00:21:11.040
and can be reviewed
later on in Stackdriver.

00:21:11.040 --> 00:21:14.010
Now I will show you Binary
Authorization in action

00:21:14.010 --> 00:21:15.970
by giving you a quick demo.

00:21:21.320 --> 00:21:24.970
In this demo, we'll
show you two use cases.

00:21:24.970 --> 00:21:28.890
One is enforcing deployment
of signed images.

00:21:28.890 --> 00:21:33.320
Second is only deploy
whitelisted third-party images.

00:21:33.320 --> 00:21:36.160
So let's start by
showing you how

00:21:36.160 --> 00:21:37.410
signature verification works.

00:21:37.410 --> 00:21:40.990
We have a pipeline here
that's built using CloudBees.

00:21:40.990 --> 00:21:45.130
In the pipeline, there are two
attesting, or signing stages--

00:21:45.130 --> 00:21:47.430
signing everything
that's built successfully

00:21:47.430 --> 00:21:49.210
or signing something
that is built

00:21:49.210 --> 00:21:53.780
from tagged or
production-ready code.

00:21:53.780 --> 00:21:55.880
For the two signing
stages, we need

00:21:55.880 --> 00:21:57.731
two corresponding
attesters created

00:21:57.731 --> 00:21:58.730
in Binary Authorization.

00:21:58.730 --> 00:22:02.450
So we have the build
attester and a tag attester.

00:22:02.450 --> 00:22:07.520
Each of the attesters has
its own private signing key.

00:22:07.520 --> 00:22:08.875
The public key is listed here.

00:22:08.875 --> 00:22:10.250
But the customer
would be the one

00:22:10.250 --> 00:22:14.330
managing the private signing
key that only the trusted stages

00:22:14.330 --> 00:22:17.550
in the build pipeline have
access to that can produce

00:22:17.550 --> 00:22:21.590
this signature or attestation.

00:22:21.590 --> 00:22:25.350
So now let's switch
to the project

00:22:25.350 --> 00:22:29.670
where the image will be
deployed and create clusters

00:22:29.670 --> 00:22:31.156
with Binary
Authorization enabled.

00:22:31.156 --> 00:22:32.280
So we'll have two clusters.

00:22:32.280 --> 00:22:37.110
We'll have a dev cluster,
where we'll deploy anything,

00:22:37.110 --> 00:22:41.700
as long as it's built
on a trusted pipeline.

00:22:41.700 --> 00:22:45.080
I'll also have a
production cluster,

00:22:45.080 --> 00:22:50.410
where I only want to deploy code
that are built from production

00:22:50.410 --> 00:22:53.770
tagged code.

00:22:53.770 --> 00:22:56.636
So let's go ahead and define
that in Binary Authorization

00:22:56.636 --> 00:22:57.135
policy.

00:23:00.320 --> 00:23:03.650
Policies are defined
in terms of attesters.

00:23:03.650 --> 00:23:05.345
So the two attesters
that I created

00:23:05.345 --> 00:23:07.880
would require the
build attester, i.e.,

00:23:07.880 --> 00:23:09.620
anything with build
with security,

00:23:09.620 --> 00:23:12.220
for the project
as a default rule.

00:23:12.220 --> 00:23:14.360
With my production
cluster, I would

00:23:14.360 --> 00:23:17.420
require both the build
attester, as well

00:23:17.420 --> 00:23:20.510
as the tag attester, a.k.a.

00:23:20.510 --> 00:23:23.980
having the tagged attestations
signifying that it's

00:23:23.980 --> 00:23:25.580
built from tagged source code.

00:23:35.170 --> 00:23:38.220
Now that I have
that defined, let's

00:23:38.220 --> 00:23:42.352
go to our build pipeline
and start a build.

00:23:45.660 --> 00:23:51.600
As you can see, that the build
runs through the attesting,

00:23:51.600 --> 00:23:54.900
to build attestations stage, but
skipped the tagged attestations

00:23:54.900 --> 00:23:57.300
stage because the
code is not tagged.

00:23:57.300 --> 00:24:04.247
So the pipeline will not add
the tagged attestation to it.

00:24:04.247 --> 00:24:06.080
So let's go into the
console and take a look

00:24:06.080 --> 00:24:11.060
at what attestations are created
for the newly created image.

00:24:11.060 --> 00:24:13.950
So we did a query.

00:24:13.950 --> 00:24:18.610
And here, the image has
one new attestation.

00:24:18.610 --> 00:24:20.760
Now let's deploy this
image to our dev cluster.

00:24:29.120 --> 00:24:31.270
Take a look at the
work load data.

00:24:31.270 --> 00:24:36.460
Yep, it was deployed successful
because dev cluster inherits

00:24:36.460 --> 00:24:38.820
the default project
rule that only

00:24:38.820 --> 00:24:41.270
requires build attestation.

00:24:41.270 --> 00:24:43.530
Now lets try to
deploy that image

00:24:43.530 --> 00:24:48.120
on the product
cluster, which requires

00:24:48.120 --> 00:24:51.960
both a build attestation, as
well as the tagged attestation.

00:24:51.960 --> 00:24:54.090
As expected, the
deployment failed

00:24:54.090 --> 00:24:58.000
because the image does not
have the tagged attestations

00:24:58.000 --> 00:25:00.940
since it was not built
from tagged code.

00:25:00.940 --> 00:25:05.190
So let's go in and mark the code
for this image as production

00:25:05.190 --> 00:25:12.620
ready and rebuild the image.

00:25:12.620 --> 00:25:15.960
As you see, the build
pipeline skips ahead

00:25:15.960 --> 00:25:18.930
and adds the tagged
attestation to the image

00:25:18.930 --> 00:25:21.270
now that it's able to find
a tag on the source code.

00:25:25.010 --> 00:25:27.970
And now let's try to deploy
the image to prod again.

00:25:49.330 --> 00:25:52.210
All right, as
expected, it succeeds

00:25:52.210 --> 00:25:56.490
because both
attestations are present.

00:25:56.490 --> 00:25:59.420
Now let's take a look at how
to run third-party images

00:25:59.420 --> 00:26:04.440
on infrastructure.

00:26:04.440 --> 00:26:07.180
We'll use Nginx as an example.

00:26:07.180 --> 00:26:10.650
So my security team has deemed
a particular version of Nginx

00:26:10.650 --> 00:26:14.250
as trustworthy and want to
make sure that everybody only

00:26:14.250 --> 00:26:18.164
deploys this version of Nginx
on the entire infrastructure.

00:26:22.120 --> 00:26:26.690
But let's try to deploy it
before we modify our deployment

00:26:26.690 --> 00:26:28.130
rule.

00:26:28.130 --> 00:26:31.760
As you can see, the
deployment failed

00:26:31.760 --> 00:26:34.820
because the images
are not built by me,

00:26:34.820 --> 00:26:37.080
so it doesn't have the
necessary attestations.

00:26:37.080 --> 00:26:41.560
Well, what we have to do is to
go into Binary Authorization

00:26:41.560 --> 00:26:47.570
and add the image
to the white list.

00:26:47.570 --> 00:26:50.200
The white list essentially
says that in addition

00:26:50.200 --> 00:26:52.820
to any images that has
the right attestation,

00:26:52.820 --> 00:26:57.310
the right signatures, also trust
these explicit list of images

00:26:57.310 --> 00:27:00.400
that may not have the
right attestation,

00:27:00.400 --> 00:27:03.260
in this case,
third party images.

00:27:03.260 --> 00:27:05.670
Now that we've added it,
let's try to deploy it again.

00:27:14.220 --> 00:27:17.950
Deployment was successful.

00:27:17.950 --> 00:27:21.000
Well, what happens if I try to
do a different version of Nginx

00:27:21.000 --> 00:27:25.010
that's different from what
my security team whitelisted

00:27:25.010 --> 00:27:26.470
in the deployment policy?

00:27:29.320 --> 00:27:31.590
That deployment fails because
it's a different digest.

00:27:35.010 --> 00:27:37.800
Here is a link to Binary
Authorization website

00:27:37.800 --> 00:27:39.120
for you to learn more.

00:27:46.990 --> 00:27:52.300
And in addition to the
integrated deployment

00:27:52.300 --> 00:27:55.000
policy enforcement provided
Binary Authorization

00:27:55.000 --> 00:27:59.200
to Kubernetes Engine, what about
Kubernetes deployment that you

00:27:59.200 --> 00:28:02.590
have on prem or elsewhere?

00:28:02.590 --> 00:28:05.410
We believe that
deployed hand controls

00:28:05.410 --> 00:28:10.270
is one of the key security
best practices for containers.

00:28:10.270 --> 00:28:13.780
That is why we provided a open
source reference implementation

00:28:13.780 --> 00:28:18.670
Kritis that will provide similar
enforcement flow for Kubernetes

00:28:18.670 --> 00:28:21.300
deployment everywhere.

00:28:21.300 --> 00:28:23.910
Kritis integrates with
Grafeas metadata API

00:28:23.910 --> 00:28:29.010
that Juan just mentioned
earlier and provides enforcement

00:28:29.010 --> 00:28:32.240
based on metadata that you
recorded in the Grafeas API.

00:28:35.260 --> 00:28:38.640
It performs the enforcement
using Kubernetes admissions

00:28:38.640 --> 00:28:39.820
controller.

00:28:39.820 --> 00:28:43.200
And it's open source
built with the community.

00:28:43.200 --> 00:28:44.840
It provides two parts.

00:28:44.840 --> 00:28:49.057
It provides the enforcement,
deployment-time enforcement,

00:28:49.057 --> 00:28:50.390
similar to Binary Authorization.

00:28:50.390 --> 00:28:56.920
It also provides a signer
that connects metadata

00:28:56.920 --> 00:28:58.720
to attestations.

00:28:58.720 --> 00:29:01.570
So in this example,
the Kritis signer

00:29:01.570 --> 00:29:06.490
would examine build or
vulnerability findings

00:29:06.490 --> 00:29:12.720
published in any Grafeas
API and apply custom logics

00:29:12.720 --> 00:29:15.720
to determine if the
findings are acceptable

00:29:15.720 --> 00:29:17.340
to your organization.

00:29:17.340 --> 00:29:20.160
If it is, it would
issue and attestation

00:29:20.160 --> 00:29:26.330
that can be used to perform
deploy-time enforcement later

00:29:26.330 --> 00:29:26.830
on.

00:29:29.470 --> 00:29:31.720
Binary Authorization
is now in beta.

00:29:31.720 --> 00:29:34.780
We just announced a
beta launch yesterday.

00:29:34.780 --> 00:29:39.730
So welcome to come and try
and join our discussion group.

00:29:39.730 --> 00:29:41.910
Let us know how you like it.

00:29:41.910 --> 00:29:43.460
As a mission,
Binary Authorization

00:29:43.460 --> 00:29:47.220
provides policy enforcement
for GKE at deploy time

00:29:47.220 --> 00:29:49.870
both in terms of
signature verification,

00:29:49.870 --> 00:29:51.940
as well as image whitelisting.

00:29:51.940 --> 00:29:55.464
We provide project- and
cluster-level policies

00:29:55.464 --> 00:29:58.170
via Grafeas and Kritis.

00:29:58.170 --> 00:30:00.720
It integrates with GCR
Vulnerability Scanning

00:30:00.720 --> 00:30:03.120
and Cloud Build.

00:30:03.120 --> 00:30:05.740
We also support Breakglass
deployment, which I did not

00:30:05.740 --> 00:30:07.330
mention in my presentation.

00:30:07.330 --> 00:30:10.570
That is when if you have
a production emergency,

00:30:10.570 --> 00:30:15.850
you are able to push through
deployments that do not

00:30:15.850 --> 00:30:21.610
meet Binary Authorization policy
specifying a Breakglass flag.

00:30:21.610 --> 00:30:23.320
And this incident
will be recorded,

00:30:23.320 --> 00:30:24.610
and you can review this later.

00:30:24.610 --> 00:30:28.090
This gives you the
flexibility to accommodate

00:30:28.090 --> 00:30:32.710
any operational anomalies
in your deployment flow.

00:30:32.710 --> 00:30:36.550
We also have partner support
from popular security tools,

00:30:36.550 --> 00:30:39.130
such as Twistlock, as well
as popular CI/CD tools,

00:30:39.130 --> 00:30:41.510
such as CloudBees and Jenkins.

00:30:41.510 --> 00:30:43.820
So integration with your
existing CI/CD pipeline

00:30:43.820 --> 00:30:45.040
should be very easy.

00:30:45.040 --> 00:30:48.070
In addition to all that, we also
provide a open source reference

00:30:48.070 --> 00:30:53.080
implementation that you can use
for your Kubernetes deployment

00:30:53.080 --> 00:30:55.940
outside of GKE.

00:30:55.940 --> 00:30:56.440
Thank you.

00:30:56.440 --> 00:30:57.490
So what's next, Maya?

00:31:01.215 --> 00:31:02.590
MAYA KACZOROWSKI:
Thanks, Sandra.

00:31:02.590 --> 00:31:04.010
So you should
definitely check out

00:31:04.010 --> 00:31:05.718
Container Registry
Vulnerability Scanning

00:31:05.718 --> 00:31:07.120
and Binary Authorization.

00:31:07.120 --> 00:31:08.620
There's lots of
other things you can

00:31:08.620 --> 00:31:10.282
do to protect your
containers as well.

00:31:10.282 --> 00:31:11.990
There's a lot of
guidance you can follow.

00:31:11.990 --> 00:31:13.656
So we've tried to
break this down by how

00:31:13.656 --> 00:31:15.010
mature your deployment is.

00:31:15.010 --> 00:31:16.510
First you have to
set up a cluster.

00:31:16.510 --> 00:31:19.330
You're going to want
some basic functionality

00:31:19.330 --> 00:31:21.860
to provide user management
and network segmentation.

00:31:21.860 --> 00:31:24.551
These include how you're
going to access kubectl,

00:31:24.551 --> 00:31:26.050
how you prevent
access to your users

00:31:26.050 --> 00:31:28.990
using RBAC, how you restrict
intra-cluster traffic

00:31:28.990 --> 00:31:31.720
with a network policy,
how you architect your app

00:31:31.720 --> 00:31:33.660
and segment your cluster
using namespaces,

00:31:33.660 --> 00:31:36.137
and how you boot strap
trust for your cluster CA.

00:31:36.137 --> 00:31:37.720
Now that you're up
and running, you're

00:31:37.720 --> 00:31:39.969
going to want to maintain
some basic security hygiene.

00:31:39.969 --> 00:31:42.129
This is about ensuring that
you're using containers

00:31:42.129 --> 00:31:43.920
to really leverage
their security benefits.

00:31:43.920 --> 00:31:47.020
This includes keeping Kubernetes
in your OS packages, images,

00:31:47.020 --> 00:31:49.360
et cetera updated
and patched, using

00:31:49.360 --> 00:31:51.040
a minimal OS for
a smaller surface

00:31:51.040 --> 00:31:52.780
attack, like
Container-optimized OS,

00:31:52.780 --> 00:31:57.070
or COS on Kubernetes engine,
using minimum IAM rules

00:31:57.070 --> 00:31:59.590
for least privilege,
private IP addresses

00:31:59.590 --> 00:32:01.900
to limit network
connectivity of your cluster,

00:32:01.900 --> 00:32:03.760
monitoring access
with audit logging,

00:32:03.760 --> 00:32:06.009
and ensuring that only the
binaries that you build

00:32:06.009 --> 00:32:06.550
are deployed.

00:32:06.550 --> 00:32:08.620
That's Binary Authorization.

00:32:08.620 --> 00:32:10.510
Once that's done,
prevent known attacks.

00:32:10.510 --> 00:32:12.510
These are common
misconfigurations in Kubernetes

00:32:12.510 --> 00:32:15.040
or known vulnerabilities that
could affect your cluster.

00:32:15.040 --> 00:32:17.320
These include disabling
the Kubernetes dashboard

00:32:17.320 --> 00:32:19.060
because it has
privileged access,

00:32:19.060 --> 00:32:21.040
disabling the default
service account token,

00:32:21.040 --> 00:32:23.530
protecting node metadata as
it may grant broader access

00:32:23.530 --> 00:32:25.220
than needed, and
scanning your images

00:32:25.220 --> 00:32:26.440
for known vulnerabilities,
for example

00:32:26.440 --> 00:32:28.060
using Container
Registry Vulnerability

00:32:28.060 --> 00:32:30.157
Scanning, and lastly,
limiting the impact

00:32:30.157 --> 00:32:31.240
of a potential compromise.

00:32:31.240 --> 00:32:33.940
This more traditional
hardening and fine tuning

00:32:33.940 --> 00:32:36.280
of your environment includes
setting a Pod Security

00:32:36.280 --> 00:32:38.470
Policy to limit what's
deployed in your environment,

00:32:38.470 --> 00:32:41.050
protecting your secrets,
sandboxing certain highly risky

00:32:41.050 --> 00:32:43.380
workloads, limiting
the identity that pods

00:32:43.380 --> 00:32:44.964
use to authenticate
to other services,

00:32:44.964 --> 00:32:47.046
and using a service mesh
to enforce strong network

00:32:47.046 --> 00:32:48.475
protection between
your services.

00:32:48.475 --> 00:32:49.650
So there's a lot here.

00:32:49.650 --> 00:32:50.650
We'll put a link
up at the end where

00:32:50.650 --> 00:32:52.483
you can find out more
about hardening advice

00:32:52.483 --> 00:32:53.350
for Kubernetes.

00:32:53.350 --> 00:32:54.831
But don't despair.

00:32:54.831 --> 00:32:56.830
There's a lot going on
in the container security

00:32:56.830 --> 00:32:59.870
space and a lot you can do to
help protect your containers.

00:32:59.870 --> 00:33:01.800
And so those are the two items
we really covered today, right?

00:33:01.800 --> 00:33:03.591
Binary Authorization
and Container Registry

00:33:03.591 --> 00:33:05.950
Vulnerability Scanning are
just one piece of a broader

00:33:05.950 --> 00:33:08.590
container security story.

00:33:08.590 --> 00:33:10.974
So in addition to what
we just covered today,

00:33:10.974 --> 00:33:12.640
why would you consider
Kubernetes Engine

00:33:12.640 --> 00:33:15.070
over running Kubernetes
yourself, from a purely

00:33:15.070 --> 00:33:16.109
security point of view?

00:33:16.109 --> 00:33:17.650
Well, first of all,
Kubernetes Engine

00:33:17.650 --> 00:33:20.672
provides some common security
best practices by default.

00:33:20.672 --> 00:33:22.630
There are more things
being built in and turned

00:33:22.630 --> 00:33:25.424
on by default which you
just kind of benefit from,

00:33:25.424 --> 00:33:27.090
which you don't have
to manage yourself.

00:33:27.090 --> 00:33:28.631
There's always going
to be applicable

00:33:28.631 --> 00:33:30.970
hardening you have to do for
your specific application.

00:33:30.970 --> 00:33:32.886
Next, a lot of these new
defaults and security

00:33:32.886 --> 00:33:35.865
patches automatically and
easily end up in your deployment

00:33:35.865 --> 00:33:37.990
because Kubernetes patches
you to the latest master

00:33:37.990 --> 00:33:39.342
version automatically.

00:33:39.342 --> 00:33:41.800
And you can also enable Node
Auto Upgrade to get the latest

00:33:41.800 --> 00:33:43.360
version running on your nodes.

00:33:43.360 --> 00:33:44.950
We talked about
containers enabling

00:33:44.950 --> 00:33:47.557
a simpler patch management
system for your environment.

00:33:47.557 --> 00:33:49.390
That's not really helpful
if you're not also

00:33:49.390 --> 00:33:50.080
patching Kubernetes.

00:33:50.080 --> 00:33:51.704
So use something like
Node Auto Upgrade

00:33:51.704 --> 00:33:53.280
to make that even
easier on yourself.

00:33:53.280 --> 00:33:55.030
And lastly, it works
with the rest of GCP.

00:33:55.030 --> 00:33:57.720
You can use IAM, audit logging,
VPCs, and other products

00:33:57.720 --> 00:34:00.220
that you're already familiar
with as part of your Kubernetes

00:34:00.220 --> 00:34:00.850
deployment.

00:34:00.850 --> 00:34:02.684
And Binary Authorization,
Container Registry

00:34:02.684 --> 00:34:04.600
Vulnerability Scanning,
they all work together

00:34:04.600 --> 00:34:07.672
as part of a broader
picture you can use on GCP.

00:34:07.672 --> 00:34:10.130
So if you want to learn more,
there's a couple links things

00:34:10.130 --> 00:34:12.260
here, our general
container security overview

00:34:12.260 --> 00:34:14.449
and then the deep dives
on the two products

00:34:14.449 --> 00:34:17.650
that we talked about today.

00:34:17.650 --> 00:34:20.350
And please stay
tuned for a live Q&amp;A.

00:34:20.350 --> 00:34:21.949
We'll be back in
less than a minute.

00:34:21.949 --> 00:34:22.449
Thanks.

00:35:59.654 --> 00:36:01.570
JUAN SEBASTIAN OVIEDO:
Welcome back, everyone.

00:36:01.570 --> 00:36:03.540
Now let's go over some
of your questions.

00:36:03.540 --> 00:36:05.940
First question that
we got was, how

00:36:05.940 --> 00:36:09.810
do I use Grafeas with Kritis?

00:36:09.810 --> 00:36:11.170
SANDRA GUO: I'll take that one.

00:36:11.170 --> 00:36:16.200
So Grafeas provides an open
source metadata format and API

00:36:16.200 --> 00:36:23.940
for users to conveniently
store and retrieve metadata

00:36:23.940 --> 00:36:25.380
associated with containers.

00:36:25.380 --> 00:36:29.050
Kritis provides the enforcement
piece of that story.

00:36:29.050 --> 00:36:30.590
So you can imagine
that you would

00:36:30.590 --> 00:36:33.840
use Grafeas to store metadata,
such as vulnerability findings

00:36:33.840 --> 00:36:36.900
or verifiable build
record that describes

00:36:36.900 --> 00:36:39.700
how your containers
are built. And then you

00:36:39.700 --> 00:36:42.090
can use Kritis to
define how you want

00:36:42.090 --> 00:36:45.150
to enforce deployment using
those information stored

00:36:45.150 --> 00:36:47.040
in Grafeas.

00:36:47.040 --> 00:36:51.750
As we mentioned earlier, you can
have a locally hosted Grafeas

00:36:51.750 --> 00:36:55.760
instance, or you can have
a Grafeas implementation

00:36:55.760 --> 00:37:01.800
as hosted by GCP, which is
the container analysis API.

00:37:01.800 --> 00:37:03.900
Kritis or Binary
Authorization works

00:37:03.900 --> 00:37:06.630
with both implementations.

00:37:06.630 --> 00:37:10.710
With Kritis, we provide
a open source signer

00:37:10.710 --> 00:37:14.220
that you can define what is
acceptable in a vulnerability

00:37:14.220 --> 00:37:20.940
finding or in a verifiable build
record that you would generate

00:37:20.940 --> 00:37:29.440
attestation only if an image has
the recommended build record,

00:37:29.440 --> 00:37:32.820
as well as vulnerability
levels, which

00:37:32.820 --> 00:37:35.040
can be then enforced and
deployed [INAUDIBLE]..

00:37:35.040 --> 00:37:37.640
So in short, Grafeas
provide the metadata piece

00:37:37.640 --> 00:37:41.760
that you can insert and
retrieve and examine metadata.

00:37:41.760 --> 00:37:44.580
Kritis provide the
deploy-time enforcement piece.

00:37:44.580 --> 00:37:45.749
And they do work together.

00:37:45.749 --> 00:37:47.040
JUAN SEBASTIAN OVIEDO: Awesome.

00:37:47.040 --> 00:37:48.000
Awesome.

00:37:48.000 --> 00:37:51.425
Our next question is, why use
a central metadata repository?

00:37:51.425 --> 00:37:54.560
And why not put this data with
the container using labels?

00:37:56.600 --> 00:37:58.200
SANDRA GUO: The
first order for that

00:37:58.200 --> 00:38:00.950
is because containers
are immutable,

00:38:00.950 --> 00:38:05.600
and labels are considered
part of a container image.

00:38:05.600 --> 00:38:10.280
So when a label change, the
identifier of a container

00:38:10.280 --> 00:38:12.180
would also change,
which is the digest.

00:38:12.180 --> 00:38:15.816
To digest the open container
is content addressable.

00:38:15.816 --> 00:38:17.930
So this doesn't
really work well when

00:38:17.930 --> 00:38:22.160
you want to attach mutable
metadata, such as image

00:38:22.160 --> 00:38:26.180
findings, vulnerability
findings, or test results

00:38:26.180 --> 00:38:29.960
that are generated after
a container is built.

00:38:29.960 --> 00:38:31.880
That is why we choose
to keep the metadata

00:38:31.880 --> 00:38:34.200
outside of the image.

00:38:34.200 --> 00:38:38.990
It will also decouple it from
the image repository and easier

00:38:38.990 --> 00:38:41.450
to integrate with
different systems

00:38:41.450 --> 00:38:45.770
that you may have in
your CI/CD workflow.

00:38:45.770 --> 00:38:47.660
Our next question's
for you, Juan.

00:38:47.660 --> 00:38:50.600
Does vulnerability
scanning cover CentOS?

00:38:50.600 --> 00:38:52.850
JUAN SEBASTIAN OVIEDO:
Currently, it doesn't cover it.

00:38:52.850 --> 00:38:55.010
But it's something that
we're actively working on.

00:38:55.010 --> 00:38:59.870
And we'll release support for
both CentOS and Red Hat soon.

00:38:59.870 --> 00:39:01.740
We'll share more details
in the near future.

00:39:01.740 --> 00:39:03.740
But it's one of the top
requests that we've received,

00:39:03.740 --> 00:39:05.281
and we're actively
working on support

00:39:05.281 --> 00:39:08.310
for those two distributions.

00:39:08.310 --> 00:39:12.090
Next question, for Sandra,
how should I manage the keys

00:39:12.090 --> 00:39:15.054
for my attestation authorities?

00:39:15.054 --> 00:39:15.720
SANDRA GUO: Yep.

00:39:15.720 --> 00:39:19.890
So you would manage
the keys yourself.

00:39:19.890 --> 00:39:22.437
We currently don't have a key
management solution integrated

00:39:22.437 --> 00:39:23.520
with Binary Authorization.

00:39:23.520 --> 00:39:28.320
So if you have an attester
that is doing the signing,

00:39:28.320 --> 00:39:32.520
that attester would have access
to the private signing key.

00:39:32.520 --> 00:39:34.950
And the public key
will be uploaded

00:39:34.950 --> 00:39:37.080
into Binary
Authorization policy so

00:39:37.080 --> 00:39:39.900
that we can use the public
key to verify the signature

00:39:39.900 --> 00:39:44.310
as the image gets deployed.

00:39:44.310 --> 00:39:48.360
But for now you'll be the one
managing the private signing

00:39:48.360 --> 00:39:50.710
keys yourself.

00:39:50.710 --> 00:39:52.520
Next one, what
partners can I use

00:39:52.520 --> 00:39:54.880
with vulnerability scanning?

00:39:54.880 --> 00:39:57.040
JUAN SEBASTIAN OVIEDO:
So currently, they're

00:39:57.040 --> 00:40:00.160
not directly exposed in the
GCR UI that I showed you.

00:40:00.160 --> 00:40:04.690
But there's work ongoing in the
cloud security command center.

00:40:04.690 --> 00:40:06.430
And there are
integrations happening

00:40:06.430 --> 00:40:08.860
from different partners,
including Twistlock,

00:40:08.860 --> 00:40:09.940
for example.

00:40:09.940 --> 00:40:11.800
And basically in
the CSCC you'll be

00:40:11.800 --> 00:40:14.050
able to access the
vulnerability findings

00:40:14.050 --> 00:40:15.490
from different providers.

00:40:15.490 --> 00:40:18.130
One provider is DCR
Vulnerability Scanning.

00:40:18.130 --> 00:40:19.980
The other providers,
for example, the ones

00:40:19.980 --> 00:40:20.980
that I mentioned before.

00:40:20.980 --> 00:40:22.896
And you'll be able to
